    {
      "id": "es-u01-l4",
      "title": "Where are you from?",
      "notes": "**¿De dónde eres?** means 'where are you from?' - literally 'from where are you?'. Spanish puts the little word **de** (from, of) first; it can't be left at the end the way English does.\nAnswer with **soy de** + place: **Soy de Australia.** The polite question is **¿De dónde es usted?**\nCountry names are capitalised as in English, but most have a Spanish shape: **Inglaterra** (England), **Estados Unidos** (the United States), **Nueva Zelanda** (New Zealand). City names usually stay as they are: Soy de Brisbane.",
      "items": [
        {"es": "¿de dónde eres?", "en": "where are you from?", "kind": "phrase", "accept_en": ["where are you from"]},
        {"es": "¿de dónde es usted?", "en": "where are you from? (formal)", "kind": "phrase", "accept_en": ["where are you from?"]},
        {"es": "soy de", "en": "I'm from", "kind": "phrase", "accept_en": ["I am from"]},
        {"es": "de", "en": "from", "kind": "word", "accept_en": ["of"]},
        {"es": "¿y usted?", "en": "and you? (formal)", "kind": "phrase", "accept_en": ["and you?", "what about you?"]},
        {"es": "Australia", "en": "Australia", "kind": "word"},
        {"es": "España", "en": "Spain", "kind": "word"},
        {"es": "México", "en": "Mexico", "kind": "word", "note": "The x sounds like an English h: 'ME-hee-ko'."},
        {"es": "Estados Unidos", "en": "the United States", "kind": "phrase", "accept_en": ["United States", "the USA", "USA", "America"]},
        {"es": "Inglaterra", "en": "England", "kind": "word"},
        {"es": "Nueva Zelanda", "en": "New Zealand", "kind": "phrase"}
      ],
